    ('32520', 0, $$Big-head Ant was a good farmer. He lived in the ground with his family.$$),
    ('32520', 1, $$They worked very hard on their farm. Ant's family lived well.$$),
    ('32520', 2, $$Pigeon was Ant's neighbour. Pigeon lived in a tree with her family.$$),
    ('32520', 3, $$Pigeon and her family did not farm. They rested all day.$$),
    ('32520', 4, $$Pigeon and her family were very good singers.$$),
    ('32520', 5, $$"Share your food? " Pigeon asked Ant. "No! Go and farm! " replied Ant.$$),
    ('32520', 6, $$Pigeon flew down and began to sing, "Ant is king! "$$),
    ('32520', 7, $$"Ant is king! Ant is king! Ant is king! " Ant loved this song.$$),
    ('32520', 8, $$After they ate, Pigeon and her family flew up to their tree.$$),
    ('32520', 9, $$The next day, Pigeon mocked Ant. "Do kings have big heads? " she laughed.$$),
    ('32520', 10, $$"You are ungrateful and lazy! " complained Ant.$$),
    ('32520', 11, $$"I am sorry! " said Pigeon, and began to sing, "Ant is king! "$$),
    ('32527', 0, $$The Moon and the Sun were once together, they loved each other and lived happily in the sky. Everything was complete.$$),
    ('32527', 1, $$They both loved each other like no other and they kept the Earth at balance.$$),
    ('32527', 2, $$The Sun kept the Earth warm while the Moon kept it stable during its rotations.$$),
    ('32527', 3, $$Until one fateful day when everything changed.$$),
    ('32527', 4, $$There was a big explosion in the sky, a big bang. The Moon and the Sun were separated from each other. Separated in both distance and time.$$),
    ('32527', 5, $$They could not see each other anymore. They were both devastated and felt incomplete.$$),
    ('32527', 6, $$They were both sad. They longed for each other's love. Their love was so strong that it transcended through time and space.$$),
    ('32527', 7, $$But all was not lost as something was about to happen, something that will change everything. Something that will change their fate.$$),
    ('32527', 8, $$Suddenly there was a force of extraction that caused them to move closer to each other every once in a while. Eventually sun the moon would be together during a process called Solar Eclipse.$$),
    ('32527', 9, $$Finally, they were together and they were very happy. It was not like before but that did not matter. What mattered was the the time and space they had.$$),
    ('32527', 10, $$During this process they did not have enough time. They made every second count. Their burning love was so strong and bright that people had to wear special glasses just to look at them in the sky. The whole world looked at them with admiration.$$),
    ('32527', 11, $$The Moon and Sun felt each other's love as time caused them to drift away from each other into the deep void space. Their love still burning through time and space. While apart, they both longed, longing for another Eclipse of Love.$$),
    ('32527', 12, $$The End.$$),
    ('32606', 0, $$We are punctuation marks. We are the superheroes found on Sentence Street! We will show you how and where to use punctuation in sentences. Let us guide you in reading and writing. We will help you to write clear and meaningful sentences, and to read punctuation with understanding.$$),
    ('32606', 1, $$We are nine punctuation marks who will introduce ourselves today. There are more of us, but you can meet them later. We hope you will ask us for help when making sentences.$$),
    ('32606', 2, $$You will find me at the start of:@a list, @or a title, @or an explanation,@or to introduce something. Call me: Colon.$$),
    ('32606', 3, $$My name is Comma. I help you to pause between thoughts, and I also separate items in a list. I love exercising, reading and playing. Would you tell me about the things you love doing?$$),
    ('32606', 4, $$Curiosity is my super power! My mission is to ask questions and find answers! Please call me Question Mark, would you?$$),
    ('32606', 5, $$I am Semicolon; I am a guard on Sentence Street. I'm a longer pause than a comma, but I'm not a full stop. I sometimes play Cupid by helping lonely clauses connect and become friends instead of strangers. I can also separate items in a long list to avoid confusion.$$),
    ('32606', 6, $$When a letter or two is missing from a word, I'll stand in for the letters. I also show possession or that something belongs to someone. Call me Apostrophe.$$),
    ('32606', 7, $$"I am Quotation Mark and my super power is direct speech! " When you see me wrapped around the words someone says, it means those are the exact words that the person spoke (or thought). I open and close quotations. Sometimes I show sarcasm.$$),
    ('32606', 8, $$Hey there! I am Exclamation Mark, the emotion mark! I express strong emotions. I express surprise and excitement! I shout, scream, laugh hysterically and wail! I am always willing to help you express your strong feelings.$$),
    ('32606', 9, $$We are three. @... 3 dots that represent missing or unsaid words. This happens when people are unsure of what to say next and don't complete their sentence. We are called ellipsis.$$),
    ('32606', 10, $$I end sentences. I am the stop indicator. Without me, everyone on Sentence Street will run out of breath and need to visit a doctor often. My name is Period, or Full Stop.$$),
    ('32606', 11, $$People like to say that Ellipsis and Period are cousins. Others say Question Mark, Exclamation Mark and Quotation Mark are siblings. There are those who say Comma and Apostrophe are twins. What do you think?$$),
    ('32648', 0, $$Udoo, Erdoo and Eryum gathered around mother. With plenty of love in her heart and her totem in her hand, mother cleared her throat and began the story: There was a little girl called Yana. Yana's aunt told her about a pot of gold at the end of a rainbow. Yana was curious about that pot. Did the pot contain gold coins, or lumps of gold in black rocks?$$),
    ('32648', 1, $$Yana imagined that if she looked at the sky long enough, she would get some answers. She smiled at the birds. She wondered how the gold got into the pot. And, where at the rainbow's end would she find the pot? Yana wished that she knew the exact spot!$$),
    ('32648', 2, $$As she thought about her rainbow quest, Yana saw a green frog jumping in the bushes. She could not resist, she jumped around with the frog for a while. Then, checking the sky, she said goodbye to the frog. Yana went on her way in search for the end of the rainbow.$$),
    ('32648', 3, $$She began to think about the rainbow's colours. In a flash, red came first to mind. She recalled the day her family almost had a bad accident because a driver didn't stop at a red light. Yana was thankful because no one was badly hurt, although they all had cuts and bruises. Everyone got a huge fright.$$),
    ('32648', 4, $$After red, Yana thought of the colour orange. She loved sweet oranges. Sweet, sour and bitter were the tastes she experienced while eating oranges. Thinking about a sour orange made Yana's mouth taste sour! Yuck! Do you know that taste?$$),
    ('32648', 5, $$Yana's thoughts moved from oranges to a bowl of dark indigo beans, and her stomach made noises. She realized that she was hungry. Her rainbow quest would have to wait for a while. It was time to eat some food!$$),
    ('32648', 6, $$She ran into her aunt's kitchen. The air carried the aroma of the fried chicken and fresh bread. Yana washed her hands and her nose led her to the food. She ate a little chicken with a large piece of bread, and then she drank water. She looked down at her dress. What a mess! Stains from the chicken, and water spilled. So she changed into a beautiful purple dress.$$),
    ('32648', 7, $$Later, Yana and her aunt went out to the park together. They lay on the grass with a book. Yana eagerly peered into the pages itching to finally know more about the hidden pot of gold. Yana's aunt chuckled at her excitement and they both burst into peals of laughter. They began to read the story together.$$),
    ('32648', 8, $$It was a tale about how the rainbow colours came to the land of Tonota, and a little boy called Mpho. For the longest time, the people of Tonota only knew the green of plants and the brown of soil. But Mpho had dreams of clouds in glorious colours! He told the elders about his dreams. They said, "If you name the colours, we can bring them to life. "$$),
    ('32648', 9, $$The elders asked for the help of rainmakers from across the land, and they gathered together. Mpho began thinking about the colours in his dreams. Then, these words came to his mind, "Red, blue, orange, yellow, purple, green, and indigo. " The colourful clouds filled the sky!$$),
    ('32648', 10, $$The clouds hung in the air, and the rainmakers formed a circle, holding hands. The rainmakers looked up to the clouds and the colours formed a beautiful arc. This was Tonata's first rainbow! People cheered and rejoiced. And just then, Mpho noticed a pot at the end of the rainbow. A pot filled with gold!$$),
    ('32648', 11, $$"What an amazing rainbow story! Now I know where to find my pot of gold! " exclaimed Yana to her aunt. Her aunt smiled, happy she shared her favourite childhood story with her niece. "But, no more rainbow quests for you! The pot of gold can only be found by accident! "$$),
    ('32648', 12, $$With those words, mother finished her rainbow tale. Udoo, Erdoo and Eryum jumped up and gave her a standing ovation. Then they held hands like the rainmakers, pretending to make their own rainbow!$$),
    ('32648', 13, $$"Mother, does every land have its own rainbow story? " asked Erdoo thoughtfully. "Yes, my lovelies," said mother. "Tonota's rainbow started with Mpho's dreams of colourful clouds. And remember the Magical Rainbow River and its rainbow ice-cream? " "Mmmm, rainbow ice-cream, that's my favourite story," said Eryum looking at mother hopefully as the children gathered around her again.$$),
    ('32654', 0, $$There lived a green frog called Bunato who had no food. He was sad and very hungry, he really needed to eat.$$),
    ('32654', 1, $$Bunato dreamed of eating the food he normally saw the humans eat but he did not know any human that would give him some food.$$),
    ('32654', 2, $$He went out to get some food for himself but it was raining heavily. He lost all hope because he knew he would not meet any human in the rain.$$),
    ('32654', 3, $$On his way, he met Usha the snake and asked him if he had some food to share with him but Usha had eaten his last meal, there was none to share.$$),
    ('32654', 4, $$Siko had no food. Bunana the monkey arrived while they spoke, they talked about where to get some food for Bunato.$$),
    ('32654', 5, $$Even Bunana had no food so Bunato decided to ask everyone for help. They could have helped but Bunato only wanted to eat bread, corn and pineapple.$$),
    ('32654', 6, $$Siko the snake decided to check with his friend Mr Busco the human for these food items on Bunato's behalf.$$),
    ('32654', 7, $$Siko was fortunate, his friend Mr Busco had some bread, pineapple and corn in his home so he gave him some to take to his friend Bunato.$$),
    ('32658', 0, $$Tortoise and Eagle were good friends. But Eagle had a bad temper. A net trapped him every time he got angry. One day he invited tortoise for launch.$$),
    ('32658', 1, $$Tortoise invited his friend Rooster. Eagle was not happy with Tortoise for bringing his friend along. @He got angry and the net appeared and trapped him!$$),
    ('32658', 2, $$When the net trapped him, he calmed down and it left.@Eagle called his friend Tortoise outside to ask him why he came with company. While they talked, Rooster heard.$$),
    ('32658', 3, $$Eagle asked Tortoise to help him check on the food cooking in the pot on the fire. Chicken came outside to talk with Eagle about what he heard them discussing.$$),
    ('32658', 4, $$It turned into a quarrel so Rooster left. Tortoise returned to check on his friends. He wondered about Rooster. Eagle told him what happened.$$),
    ('32658', 5, $$Eagle was so upset with Tortoise that he wanted to fight his friend. The net appeared again. Tortoise left in a storm to search fro his friend Rooster.$$),
    ('32658', 6, $$Tortoise did not see his friend Rooster so he came back and tried to settle with his friend Eagle. Sadly, they quarreled some more.$$),
    ('32658', 7, $$While they were talking, they were summoned to appear at the palace. Rooster had reported the matter to the King who helped them settle the case and all was well again.$$),
    ('32659', 0, $$Ekene and Buma are friends who loved to play were outside. One day,while they played, Buma's mother called them and sent them on a quick errand.$$),
    ('32659', 1, $$They both decided to have a race on their way so that they could return sooner than if they walked.$$),
    ('32659', 2, $$They delivered the message to the person and decided to walk back home so that they could have more fun looking at the lovely buildings.$$),
    ('32659', 3, $$They got distracted with fun they were having and forgot to run back home. While they stopped to talk they saw some of their friends playing.$$),
    ('32659', 4, $$They stopped to decide whether they should join the children playing football in the field or go home. The thought the football game was more fun so they stayed to play.$$),
    ('32659', 5, $$They ran into the field and joined their friends. they ran and ran and ran. It was fun playing football in the sun.$$),
    ('32659', 6, $$Because they scored a lot of goals and were having fun, they forgot that Buma's mother was still waiting to hear from them. But the game ended and it was time to go home.$$),
    ('32659', 7, $$They ran home panting. Mother was waiting for them at the door. She was not happy that they left her waiting. They apologized and she forgave them.$$),
    ('32660', 0, $$Elephant and Hare are friends who like playing games but are now forgetting to have fun but are always competing.@They decided to compete at a game of football.$$),
    ('32660', 1, $$Elephant started as the goal keeper and hare started as a player. Hare scored many goals and danced in joy. He was a winner.$$),
    ('32660', 2, $$Next the Hare was the goal keeper and the Elephant became the player. Elephant scored more goals because hare was smaller and did not fill up the post like Elephant did.$$),
    ('32660', 3, $$Hare was so upset that he challenged Elephant to a race. Elephant thought he could win the race so he accepted the challenge.$$),
    ('32660', 4, $$Hare was so swift that he got to the finish line while Elephant was still at the starting line. Hare danced for joy and gloated, this made Elephant very sad.$$),
    ('32660', 5, $$Elephant decided that the person who could stand the longest on fire would be declared a final winner. Hare accepted the challenge but was afraid.$$),
    ('32660', 6, $$So Elephant stood in the fire first, the fire was so intense that Elephant started to cry, he did not want to continue with the competition anymore. Hare agreed.$$),
    ('32660', 7, $$Hare ran to get Elephant some water and first aid for his friend. They both agreed that it was foolishness to continue competing instead of having fun.$$),
    ('32661', 0, $$The animals gathered at the town square to talk about the drought.$$),
    ('32661', 1, $$Fox, hare and donkey decided to go to the well across the valley of Ajalango.$$),
    ('32661', 2, $$Fox, Hare and donkey prepared for their journey picking out cloths that will serve them.$$),
    ('32661', 3, $$Fox, Hare and donkey set out on their journey with a big bowl.$$),
    ('32661', 4, $$As they walked on, they saw an ant gathering wood. They asked him if he knew where they could find water. He asked them to follow him.$$),
    ('32661', 5, $$They followed him. Ant met an old man leaning on a stick. He asked him if he could help with water for his friends. The old man said, "yes. "$$),
    ('32661', 6, $$The old man leaning on a stick gave the ant and his colony water. Ant was worried about his new friends. He asked the old man for some more water.$$),
    ('32661', 7, $$The ants beckoned on all the animals to assemble so they can all share from the generosity of the old man leaning on a stick.$$),
    ('32904', 0, $$Living things are able to move. Animals can move their limbs to run away from danger. Plants can move their flowers and leaves to the sun. Children can move by hopping on one leg.$$),
    ('32904', 1, $$All living things respire or breathe turning food into energy. Animals and humans eat food and breathe in oxygen to create energy and breathe out a gas called carbon dioxide. When we run we breathe in and out much faster. Plants change the energy they get from the carbon dioxide taken in through their leaves from sunlight into oxygen.$$),
    ('32904', 2, $$Living things respond to the outside world. We call this sensitivity. Humans make fires when they are cold. Plants wilt when they do not get water. Elephants swim in rivers when they are hot.$$),
    ('32904', 3, $$Living things have babies to survive. @Fish, frogs and birds lay eggs. Plants and trees produce seeds to make new plants and trees. Humans have babies.$$),
    ('32904', 4, $$All living things eat food. Some animals eat grass, others eat meat. Vegetables need to be planted in soil and need sun and water to grow. Food gives us energy.$$),
    ('32904', 5, $$Living things need to get rid of waste products. In animals, kidneys and lungs are some of the ways in which waste products are released. Snakes often shed all their skin at one time and it comes off as if they have taken off a coat. Plants have small openings on their leaves called stomata to release oxygen.$$),
    ('32904', 6, $$All living things grow until they have reached their full size, strength, and with the ability to make new babies. Growth is also important to replace damaged cells, like when we fall and hurt ourselves.$$),
    ('32904', 7, $$The seven life processes are movement, respiration, sensitivity, reproduction, nutrition, excretion and growth. If something can do all these seven life processes, they are alive!$$),
    ('32920', 0, $$The wind howled like a wolf. Salemka'an ran out with her kite to play.$$),
    ('32920', 1, $$The wind played with her hair. The wind played with her dress. Soon, the wind grew stronger and so she lost@her kite.$$),
    ('32920', 2, $$The wind continued to blow stronger. Salemka'an ran to get the clothes from the@line.$$),
    ('32920', 3, $$The wind was faster than her. It swirled and churned. It carried her kite and clothes. It carried the house, the trees and even the leaves.$$),
    ('32920', 4, $$The wind churned and weaved them all together into a vortex.$$),
    ('32920', 5, $$Then the wind began to calm. It dropped some trees. Salemka'an's kite was stuck to a branch.$$),
    ('32920', 6, $$The wind tossed her kite. It tossed some leaves around.$$),
    ('32920', 7, $$The wind dropped Salemka'an from the sky along with a tree. She twirled and twirled.$$),
    ('32920', 8, $$She held on to the roots because she felt woozy,@woozy.$$),
    ('32920', 9, $$She staggered up, still holding on to the roots. She looked around for everything the wind@weaved into a vortex.$$),
    ('32920', 10, $$She saw her house. She saw a few leaves and tree trunks. She didn't see her kite but walked home all the@same.$$),
    ('32920', 11, $$Salemka'an knew that the wind would blow again. "I'll see my kite again," she thought.$$),
    ('32921', 0, $$Mother has a shop. She looks after her shop, her customers, her suppliers, and her family. She says she really misses our father when he travels!$$),
    ('32921', 1, $$Her phone rings almost non-stop. Customers call to ask prices, and to place their orders. They call to trace their orders, and to complain about their orders.$$),
    ('32921', 2, $$We often go to her shop in the afternoons. We tell her about school. But our conversations are always interrupted by customers eager for her attention.$$),
    ('32921', 3, $$We have fun with children who come to the shop with their parents. Sometimes, mother has to serve as referee as well as help customers. I think that she has super powers!$$),
    ('32921', 4, $$When we become too noisy, mother says, "Now sit quietly and watch TV. " She always adds, "But only for 30 minutes, while we take a break! "$$),
    ('32921', 5, $$If we don't have schoolwork, sometimes mother asks me or my older sister to help her. I am not always ready to help. But I do it because I know she works so hard.$$),
    ('32921', 6, $$As we walk home from the shop with the sun setting, we talk about our day. We have to hurry to keep up with her, but we love walking and talking with mother.$$),
    ('32921', 7, $$She makes sure that we do homework, and have time to play. She makes sure that we eat vegetables and fruits, and drink water. When we are sick, she cares for us.$$),
    ('32921', 8, $$At night, mother continues to work at home. There is always work from the shop. Sometimes she falls asleep at the table!$$),
    ('32921', 9, $$Father makes sure that mother has time to relax, and he helps around the house. "You're a Super Mummy! " he says. "And you're my Super Super! " smiles mother.$$),
    ('32941', 0, $$It was night time and little Lamb could not go to sleep. He then said to his Mom, "Mom, can you please tell me a story? ". "Okay", replied Mom. "I'll tell you the story about the Leopard".$$),
    ('32941', 1, $$"A long time ago, a Leopard came to the farm. We could see him standing on top of a rock outside. Our parents urged us not to go outside the farm", said Mom. "Then what happened? ", asked the little Lamb.$$),
    ('32941', 2, $$"One of my friends was running after a ball that went outside the farm. She went out to fetch it and she was never to be seen again. The Leopard took her", said Mom.$$),
    ('32941', 3, $$"I'm so sorry such a bad thing happened to your friend, Mom", said the little Lamb. The little Lamb was now sad. He could just imagine one of his friends disappearing, he didn't like the feeling.$$),
    ('32941', 4, $$Little Lamb climbed on to the small opening of the barn they were sleeping in and gazed into the far end of the dark land. "Mom, do you think the Leopard is still out there? ", asked the little Lamb.$$),
    ('32941', 5, $$"No", replied Mom. "Since that incident, no one has seen it or it's tracks near the farm", she added. Little Lamb was now a bit better knowing that him and his friends are safe.$$),
    ('32941', 6, $$The next day, little Lamb was walking alone in the woods and he got a little far than he usually did and to his surprise, he saw a baby Leopard. He immediately got frightened and kept his distance.$$),
    ('32941', 7, $$"Hello", said the baby Leopard. "Would you like to play with me? " Little Lamb thoroughly analysed the baby Leopard and gently said "No. " Little Lamb started to walk back home. On the way, he pondered why the baby Leopard wanted to play with him.$$),
    ('32941', 8, $$Baby Leopard got home later that night. He said, "Mom, something weird happened today". "What happened", asked Mom. "I was in the woods and I saw a Lamb and I asked him to play with me and he said No! "$$),
    ('32941', 9, $$"I think I know why", said mother Leopard, with a sad face. "Long time ago, when I was just above your age, I went to their village looking to make friends with some of the sheep there", she added.$$),
    ('32941', 10, $$"I couldn't get in the farm because it was gated so I stood outside on a rock hoping for one of them to notice me. Eventually one of the Lambs came outside chasing after a ball and a human came and took her"$$),
    ('32941', 11, $$"Took her? Took her where? ", asked baby Lamb. "To the abattoir", replied mother Leopard. "When she got taken I ran because I was scared. Since then, they have been blaming me for her disappearance", she added.$$),
    ('32941', 12, $$"I'm so sorry that you're being blamed for something you didn't do", said baby Leopard. "It's, okay", replied mother Leopard. Baby Leopard now understood why the Lamb didn't want to play with him. But he thought of making things right.$$),
    ('32941', 13, $$The next day, baby Leopard went close to where he met the Lamb and climbed on a small tree. He was hoping for the Lamb to show up. To his luck, the Lamb did. Baby Leopard explained what happened on that fateful day. "It all makes sense now", replied little Lamb.$$),
    ('32941', 14, $$Baby Leopard got down from the tree and they both started playing together. They played all the games they could think of, including tag, but they mostly jumped up and down. They were both happy about their new friendship but soon had to leave for home.$$),
    ('32941', 15, $$Little Lamb was so excited when he got home. "Mom, guess what! ". "What? ", replied Mom. "I made a friend today. He is a Leopard", said little Lamb. "A LEOPARD! ", shouted Mom. "Mom, relax, let me explain", said little Lamb.$$),
    ('32941', 16, $$Little Lamb told his Mom what baby Leopard had told him. "It all makes sense now. I have been suspecting this for a while now and it's confirmed", said Mom. Little Lamb couldn't wait for the next day so he could see his new friend.$$),
    ('32941', 17, $$The two new friends met again at the same spot and continued doing what they know best: playing. The End!$$)
  ) AS v(source_id, page_index, text)
  JOIN books b
    ON b.source_platform = 'african_storybook' AND b.source_id = v.source_id
ON CONFLICT (book_id, page_index) DO NOTHING;

-- ───────── [적재검증] 트랜잭션 안 ─────────
-- (c) 적재 후 행 수 — 기대 543
SELECT count(*) AS rows_after FROM book_text bt
  JOIN books b ON b.id = bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('31836', '31892', '31898', '31900', '31901', '31944', '31945', '31946', '31947', '31948', '31949', '31995', '32041', '32045', '32179', '32214', '32228', '32340', '32355', '32356', '32357', '32380', '32382', '32386', '32391', '32408', '32410', '32413', '32416', '32431', '32435', '32446', '32460', '32482', '32487', '32520', '32527', '32606', '32648', '32654', '32658', '32659', '32660', '32661', '32904', '32920', '32921', '32941');
-- (d) 조인 실패로 누락된 source_id — 기대 0행
SELECT v.source_id FROM (VALUES ('31836'), ('31892'), ('31898'), ('31900'), ('31901'), ('31944'), ('31945'), ('31946'), ('31947'), ('31948'), ('31949'), ('31995'), ('32041'), ('32045'), ('32179'), ('32214'), ('32228'), ('32340'), ('32355'), ('32356'), ('32357'), ('32380'), ('32382'), ('32386'), ('32391'), ('32408'), ('32410'), ('32413'), ('32416'), ('32431'), ('32435'), ('32446'), ('32460'), ('32482'), ('32487'), ('32520'), ('32527'), ('32606'), ('32648'), ('32654'), ('32658'), ('32659'), ('32660'), ('32661'), ('32904'), ('32920'), ('32921'), ('32941')) AS v(source_id)
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM books b
     WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id=v.source_id);
-- (e) source 라벨 분포 — 기대 manifest_txt_v1 1종 / 543행
SELECT bt.source, count(*) FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('31836', '31892', '31898', '31900', '31901', '31944', '31945', '31946', '31947', '31948', '31949', '31995', '32041', '32045', '32179', '32214', '32228', '32340', '32355', '32356', '32357', '32380', '32382', '32386', '32391', '32408', '32410', '32413', '32416', '32431', '32435', '32446', '32460', '32482', '32487', '32520', '32527', '32606', '32648', '32654', '32658', '32659', '32660', '32661', '32904', '32920', '32921', '32941')
  GROUP BY bt.source;
-- (f) page_index 축 검증 — 권마다 0부터 연속이어야 한다. 기대 0행(위반 없음)
SELECT b.source_id, min(bt.page_index) AS mn, max(bt.page_index) AS mx, count(*) AS n
  FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('31836', '31892', '31898', '31900', '31901', '31944', '31945', '31946', '31947', '31948', '31949', '31995', '32041', '32045', '32179', '32214', '32228', '32340', '32355', '32356', '32357', '32380', '32382', '32386', '32391', '32408', '32410', '32413', '32416', '32431', '32435', '32446', '32460', '32482', '32487', '32520', '32527', '32606', '32648', '32654', '32658', '32659', '32660', '32661', '32904', '32920', '32921', '32941')
  GROUP BY b.source_id HAVING min(bt.page_index) <> 0 OR max(bt.page_index) <> count(*)-1;
-- (g) 빈 면 행 수 — 기대 0 (D7: 빈 면도 text='' 로 적재)
SELECT count(*) AS empty_rows FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('31836', '31892', '31898', '31900', '31901', '31944', '31945', '31946', '31947', '31948', '31949', '31995', '32041', '32045', '32179', '32214', '32228', '32340', '32355', '32356', '32357', '32380', '32382', '32386', '32391', '32408', '32410', '32413', '32416', '32431', '32435', '32446', '32460', '32482', '32487', '32520', '32527', '32606', '32648', '32654', '32658', '32659', '32660', '32661', '32904', '32920', '32921', '32941') AND bt.text = '';

-- ───────── [종료] ─────────
-- (c)~(g)가 전부 기대값과 일치하면 아래 ROLLBACK; 을 COMMIT; 으로 바꿔 타이핑한다.
ROLLBACK;
